0"} {"question" "q-337259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}({x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})=\\frac{2x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{isn't}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ $\\text{isn't}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-337259" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337259" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "-y\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337259" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_explicit_ans