e" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337523" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4xy+3x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-(4xy+3x))=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{1}(-4x-0) \\\\  & =-4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -4x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot (-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52