al/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337161" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337161" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}(-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0"] ["math" "{e}^{-4x}(-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0"] ["math" "{e}^{2{x}^{2}}(-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0"] ["math" "{e}^{4x}(-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337161" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}(-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{3})=6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xy)=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}-(-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\frac{1}{2y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}(2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-337619" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337619" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337619" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/