loper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337707" "field" "field-7" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}"] ["math" "{e}^{-({y}^{3}+1)}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3y}"] ["math" "{y}^{3}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337707" "field" "field-7" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337707" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337707" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({y}^{3}+1)(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0"] ["math" "{e}^{-({y}^{3}+1)}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0"] ["math" "{e}^{-3y}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337707" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\nxy\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=xy,\\,Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}-5{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(yx\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-5y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{y}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}-5{y}^{2}=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}}-5y=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338070" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3x+{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+3.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+3)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=3y,\\,G(x)={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "x"] ["math" "{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+x=c"] ["math" "{x}^{3}+3y=c"] ["math" "{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338027" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 2/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}+y)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{y}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{2}=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}-y=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-338870" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{x}"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy=c"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2}+xy=c"] ["math" "{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}+y=c"] ["math" "{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337096" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y=c"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}[(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two expressions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64e27fff88cb729c782b2da64f3aa25f52937c98305c0cd7f9d19d2a6e4597ea"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+y=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "q-337637" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b900863b000acb74914637309952b4bb3e7b99973b8d5caa337c0a75eefa40"} {"question" "e-20896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation \n\n$$\n(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-20896.json" "file_sha256" "2af26643d12ecc20215d379cf3448baf922b1e6f23f5f6a32b97e40a0edaba02"} {"question" "e-20896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-20896.json" "file_sha256" "2af26643d12ecc20215d379cf3448baf922b1e6f23f5f6a32b97e40a0edaba02"} {"question" "e-20935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nx\\,y' = 4y - 3x\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-20935.json" "file_sha256" "d008e0f1fba5c02b8b000d96d81e6ccd3cedcc2230ec768a6cbace0d7a0c6990"} {"question" "e-20935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x  + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y  =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x}(-4-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{5}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|) \\\\  & ={x}^{-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) =  I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-20935.json" "file_sha256" "d008e0f1fba5c02b8b000d96d81e6ccd3cedcc2230ec768a6cbace0d7a0c6990"} {"question" "e-21772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-21772.json" "file_sha256" "59f290290f3b57a27b942fd3e1df9a3c6b8277a0ccf99568f9e0b3b0c4446dc7"} {"question" "e-21772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =  y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) =  xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(2y-y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -({y}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-21772.json" "file_sha256" "59f290290f3b57a27b942fd3e1df9a3c6b8277a0ccf99568f9e0b3b0c4446dc7"} {"question" "e-20898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-20898.json" "file_sha256" "83f968052da8ecbd907eb727847cf68f49559941ac20d7f74fc5576461c06d84"} {"question" "e-20898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061603/example-20898.json" "file_sha256" "83f968052da8ecbd907eb727847cf68f49559941ac20d7f74fc5576461c06d84"}] "usage" {"e-20935" [] "q-337133" [] "e-21772" [] "q-337096" [] "e-20898" [] "e-20896" []} "basis" 2026 "retractions" [[17592186217752 :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$xdy - ydx = 0$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x, y) = \\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(x, y) = \\frac{y}{x^{3}}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{3}}dx + \\frac{y}{x^{2}}dy$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186217752 :question/answer-fields 17592186216587] [17592186217750 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$(\\frac{6y^{2}}{x} + 4y^{3})dx + (4y + 3xy^{2} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{3}{x}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$P(x, y) = \\frac{6y^{2}}{x} + 4y^{3}, Q(x, y) = 4y + 3xy^{2} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."] [17592186216929 :question/problem "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation\n$(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$"] [17592186216929 :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{xy^{3}}((x^{2}y - y^{3})dx - x^{3}dy) &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{y^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x})dx - \\frac{x^{2}}{y^{3}}dy &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =-\\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x}) =-\\frac{2x}{y^{3}} \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{3}}) =-\\frac{2x}{y^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}\n$$\n\n$\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."] [17592186216931 :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n$x\\,y' = 4y - 3x$"] [17592186216931 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x - 4y) =-4 \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}x = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) &= \\frac{1}{x}(-4 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= \\exp (∫ - \\frac{5}{x}dx) \\\\\n&= \\exp (-5\\ln \\mid x \\mid) \\\\\n&= x^{-5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0\n$$"] [17592186216952 :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0$"] [17592186216952 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2}) = 2y \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) = y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) &= \\frac{1}{y^{2}}(2y - y) \\\\\n&= y^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(y) &= \\exp (∫ - (y^{-1})dy) \\\\\n&= \\exp (-\\ln y) \\\\\n&= y^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}\n$$"] [17592186216930 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation."] [17592186216930 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x$.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫x^{-1}dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx[(x + y^{2})dx + (xy + \\frac{2}{x})dy] &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + xy^{2})_(⏟)_(P^(~))dx + (x^{2}y + 2)_(⏟)_(Q^(~))dy &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad \\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x$.\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y$, which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P^(~)(x, y)dx + F(y) \\\\\n&= ∫(x^{2} + xy^{2})dx + F(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{2} + F(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x$, which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q^(~)(x, y)dy + G(x) \\\\\n&= ∫(x^{2}y + 2)dy + G(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{2} + 2y + G(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186216568 17592186216569 17592186216570 17592186216571 17592186216573 17592186216574 17592186216575 17592186216576 17592186216588 17592186216589 17592186216590 17592186216591] "immutable_fields" [[17592186216567 :answer-field/id] [17592186216567 :answer-field/key] [17592186216567 :answer-field/type] [17592186216572 :answer-field/id] [17592186216572 :answer-field/key] [17592186216572 :answer-field/type] [17592186216587 :answer-field/id] [17592186216587 :answer-field/key] [17592186216587 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
